    {
      "id": "4GPzkGBGOLX5rV_NWYoHx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1287.5593069186605,
      "y": -510.82460509666447,
      "width": 746.8994750976562,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aT",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2122889628,
      "version": 250,
      "versionNonce": 369612036,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1783718939466,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n* The component should be generic enough to be usable by different\nwebsites.\n* The input field UI and search results UI should be customizable.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n* The component should be generic enough to be usable by different websites.\n* The input field UI and search results UI should be customizable.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ghuKePU2wE8sH080E5ZB9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1473.857252959651,
      "y": -374.32909641370213,
      "width": 1032.69921875,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 903068444,
      "version": 401,
      "versionNonce": 326674748,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1783718924611,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Questions\n1. What kind of results should be support (text, image, media/media with text)\n2. What devices will this component used (laptio/tablets/mobile)\n3. support fuzzy search \n(search technique that finds text matches even when the user query does not perfectly match the data)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Questions\n1. What kind of results should be support (text, image, media/media with text)\n2. What devices will this component used (laptio/tablets/mobile)\n3. support fuzzy search \n(search technique that finds text matches even when the user query does not perfectly match the data)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2hVrHIvCfECXyuMLiAh_e",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1279.8561146142679,
      "y": 726.8395089727667,
      "width": 1980,
      "height": 2175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aX",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 800002460,
      "version": 6902,
      "versionNonce": 1300358204,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1783725556540,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\n\n\n\n\n\n----------------------------------------------\n* Network\n    * user makes changes to the query while there's a pending network request? \n      If there are multiple pending network requests, we will need to be mindful not to display results for a previous search query\n    * We cannot rely on return order of network responses from server because earlier request might complete later than request fired after\n    * There are 2 ways to know which requests's response we should diplay\n       1. Attach timestamp for reach request, only display the result of latest request (not latest response). \n       2. Save the results in object/map, keyed by query string  ==> option 2 is better for storing caching response for each query, more deterministic\n(Not advisable to abort request/Abortcontroller or discard response since server would received and processed the request and return the data back to the client)\n* Failed request/retries\n     * Server request can sometimes fail, due to flaky internet connection\n     * Component can automatically retry firing the query. In case server is indeed offline, and we concern about overloading it, we could use exponential backoff strategy\n* OFffline usage\n     * If device entirely lost its network connection, there's not a lot we could do. \n     * Read purely from cache (not useful if cache empty)\n     * Not fire any request so not waste an CPU cycle.\n     * UX: indicate in component somewhere there's no network connection.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------\n* Cache: O(1) to retrieve result. \nOption1: good for short lived website, even though there is duplicated data, user unlikely to search so often that memory can become issues\nCache is cleared/reset when user click on specific search result\nconst cache = {\n    fa: [\n      {type: 'organization', text: 'Facebook'},\n      {type: 'organization', text: 'FasTrak'},\n      {type: 'text', text: 'family'...}\n    ],\n};\n\nOption2: more long live application, however, caching too long might be a bad idea as stale results take memory without being useful\nto save space, we could also normalize the cache\nconst results: [\n   {id: 1, text: 'Facebook', ...},\n   {id: 2: text: 'Fastrack', ...}\n],\nconst cache = {\n   fa: [1, 2, ...],\n   fac: [1, 2, 3...]\n};\n\n==> Caching is space/time tradeoff, where memory space to save processing time. Dedepending type of application to determine the usefulness of cache (search vs. network vs. strock)\n-------------------------------------------------\n\n*** Performance -- refers to client side performance\n-- Loading speed: need to depends on how fast server returns response, but with caching on client side, we can show results for previous queries, can go a step further and\nuse cache results for future queries if there is match\n-- Debounce/throttling: limit number of network requests can be fired, reduce server load and CPU processing\n-- Memory usage: long-lived pages might have autocomplete component that acccumulate too many results, purging the cache and free up memory is essential here.\nThe purging can be done when the browser is idle, or when total memory/number of cache entires exceed certain threshold\nVirtualized list: only render the nodes that are visible and recycle the DOM nodes instead of creating a new ones\n\n\n*** Scrolling\n-- Intersection Observer: run on secondary thread, ensure main thread not blocked. \n-- Window.scroll: can be easier implementation, however, require additional custom code to achieve performance comparable with Intersection Observer\n\n-------------------------------------\n\n*** Memory optimization ***\n-- Long lived pages might have autocomplete components that accumulate too many results in the cache & hog memory\nPurging the cache and freeing up memory is essential for such pages. The purging can be done when the browser is idle or when\nthe total memory/number of cache entries exceed a certain threshold\n*** Virtual list\n-- If the result contains many items, rendering that many DOM nodes in browser would cost lost of memory \nand slow down browser. List virtualization is technique can be used to help component retain performance at scale\n-- rendering what is visible to the user. The number of elements that are rendered at first is\n a very small subset of the entire list and the \"window\" of visible content moves when the user continues to scroll. \nThis improves both the rendering and scrolling performance of the list.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\n\n\n\n\n\n----------------------------------------------\n* Network\n    * user makes changes to the query while there's a pending network request? \n      If there are multiple pending network requests, we will need to be mindful not to display results for a previous search query\n    * We cannot rely on return order of network responses from server because earlier request might complete later than request fired after\n    * There are 2 ways to know which requests's response we should diplay\n       1. Attach timestamp for reach request, only display the result of latest request (not latest response). \n       2. Save the results in object/map, keyed by query string  ==> option 2 is better for storing caching response for each query, more deterministic\n(Not advisable to abort request/Abortcontroller or discard response since server would received and processed the request and return the data back to the client)\n* Failed request/retries\n     * Server request can sometimes fail, due to flaky internet connection\n     * Component can automatically retry firing the query. In case server is indeed offline, and we concern about overloading it, we could use exponential backoff strategy\n* OFffline usage\n     * If device entirely lost its network connection, there's not a lot we could do. \n     * Read purely from cache (not useful if cache empty)\n     * Not fire any request so not waste an CPU cycle.\n     * UX: indicate in component somewhere there's no network connection.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------\n* Cache: O(1) to retrieve result. \nOption1: good for short lived website, even though there is duplicated data, user unlikely to search so often that memory can become issues\nCache is cleared/reset when user click on specific search result\nconst cache = {\n    fa: [\n      {type: 'organization', text: 'Facebook'},\n      {type: 'organization', text: 'FasTrak'},\n      {type: 'text', text: 'family'...}\n    ],\n};\n\nOption2: more long live application, however, caching too long might be a bad idea as stale results take memory without being useful\nto save space, we could also normalize the cache\nconst results: [\n   {id: 1, text: 'Facebook', ...},\n   {id: 2: text: 'Fastrack', ...}\n],\nconst cache = {\n   fa: [1, 2, ...],\n   fac: [1, 2, 3...]\n};\n\n==> Caching is space/time tradeoff, where memory space to save processing time. Dedepending type of application to determine the usefulness of cache (search vs. network vs. strock)\n-------------------------------------------------\n\n*** Performance -- refers to client side performance\n-- Loading speed: need to depends on how fast server returns response, but with caching on client side, we can show results for previous queries, can go a step further and\nuse cache results for future queries if there is match\n-- Debounce/throttling: limit number of network requests can be fired, reduce server load and CPU processing\n-- Memory usage: long-lived pages might have autocomplete component that acccumulate too many results, purging the cache and free up memory is essential here.\nThe purging can be done when the browser is idle, or when total memory/number of cache entires exceed certain threshold\nVirtualized list: only render the nodes that are visible and recycle the DOM nodes instead of creating a new ones\n\n\n*** Scrolling\n-- Intersection Observer: run on secondary thread, ensure main thread not blocked. \n-- Window.scroll: can be easier implementation, however, require additional custom code to achieve performance comparable with Intersection Observer\n\n-------------------------------------\n\n*** Memory optimization ***\n-- Long lived pages might have autocomplete components that accumulate too many results in the cache & hog memory\nPurging the cache and freeing up memory is essential for such pages. The purging can be done when the browser is idle or when\nthe total memory/number of cache entries exceed a certain threshold\n*** Virtual list\n-- If the result contains many items, rendering that many DOM nodes in browser would cost lost of memory \nand slow down browser. List virtualization is technique can be used to help component retain performance at scale\n-- rendering what is visible to the user. The number of elements that are rendered at first is\n a very small subset of the entire list and the \"window\" of visible content moves when the user continues to scroll. \nThis improves both the rendering and scrolling performance of the list.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "SUlgd-_QoZkA_s5OS9hOm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1300.8284267391039,
      "y": 2673.780958918849,
      "width": 859.7642822265625,
      "height": 919.8989425099127,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aa",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1904455223,
      "version": 1669,
      "versionNonce": 659227964,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1783725560338,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\n\n\n\n   * Long lived pages might have autocomplete components\n that accumulate too many results in the cache & hog memory\nPurging the cache and freeing up memory is essential for such\npages. The purging can be done when the browser is idle or when\nthe total memory/number of cache entries exceed a certain\nthreshold\n * Virtual list\n * If the results contains many item, rendering that many\nDOM nodes in the browser woul cost lost of memory and slow\ndown the brpwser. List virtualization is technique can be used\nto help component retain performance at scale.\n\n* List virtualization, or \"windowing\", \nis the concept of only rendering what is visible to the user. \nThe number of elements that are rendered at first is\n a very small subset of the entire list and the \"window\"\n of visible content moves when the user continues to scroll. \nThis improves both the rendering \nand scrolling performance of the list.\n\n\n ",
      "fontSize": 27.25626496325667,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\n\n\n\n   * Long lived pages might have autocomplete components\n that accumulate too many results in the cache & hog memory\nPurging the cache and freeing up memory is essential for such\npages. The purging can be done when the browser is idle or when\nthe total memory/number of cache entries exceed a certain\nthreshold\n * Virtual list\n * If the results contains many item, rendering that many\nDOM nodes in the browser woul cost lost of memory and slow\ndown the brpwser. List virtualization is technique can be used\nto help component retain performance at scale.\n\n* List virtualization, or \"windowing\", \nis the concept of only rendering what is visible to the user. \nThe number of elements that are rendered at first is\n a very small subset of the entire list and the \"window\"\n of visible content moves when the user continues to scroll. \nThis improves both the rendering \nand scrolling performance of the list.\n\n\n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "s7ua7V-RXEmWAPRDuw4Ss",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 284.61078086306105,
      "y": -783.7142893484211,
      "width": 1327.119140625,
      "height": 150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ag",
